From a6d303df1cf68305205a6cbf65fea5cd68b0d4be Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Stephano Cetola <stephano@cetola.net>
Date: Wed, 8 Jul 2026 17:30:00 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 06/14] spi: rockchip: restore bus_num alias lookup via
 DEVICE_ID_SINGLE
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The line ctlr->bus_num = pdev->id was commented out, leaving bus_num
at 0 (from kzalloc). spi_register_controller() only runs the alias
lookup when bus_num < 0, with bus_num = 0 the lookup is skipped and
every Rockchip SPI controller claims bus 0.

On RK3588S, spi@feb20000 (the rk806 PMIC SPI, aliased as spi2) is
enabled and probed before the gpio-spi master. With both stealing
bus 0, spi_get_controller(0) returns the PMIC controller instead of
the intended gpio-spi RP2040 bus. The barebox spi command then does
a write+read through the Rockchip SPI driver, which programmes the
hardware in XFM_RO (RX-only) mode for the read phase, in that mode
dummy TXDR writes don't generate clock pulses, the RX FIFO never
fills, and rockchip_spi_pio's while(1) loop never exits → boot hang.

Fix: set ctlr->bus_num = DEVICE_ID_SINGLE (-1) so the alias lookup
always runs. spi@feb20000 gets its correct alias id (spi2 = bus 2)
and spi_get_controller(0) returns the gpio-spi master as intended.

Note: the barebox spi shell command only supports bus-number lookup
(spi_get_controller(bus)), not DT-path identification. The correct
long-term fix is either to extend the spi command to accept device
names, or to implement the RP2040 init in C using of_find_device_by_node().
This patch is a pragmatic workaround for the MNT Pocket Reform RCM5.
---
 drivers/spi/spi-rockchip.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/spi/spi-rockchip.c b/drivers/spi/spi-rockchip.c
index 1e81e9393f..e65a2d2e44 100644
--- a/drivers/spi/spi-rockchip.c
+++ b/drivers/spi/spi-rockchip.c
@@ -514,7 +514,7 @@ static int rockchip_spi_probe(struct device *dev)
 		goto err_put_ctlr;
 	}
 
-//	ctlr->bus_num = pdev->id;
+	ctlr->bus_num = DEVICE_ID_SINGLE;
 //	ctlr->mode_bits = SPI_CPOL | SPI_CPHA | SPI_LOOP | SPI_LSB_FIRST;
 
 	/*
